  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Sat Jun 20 2026\n\nMain weather story will be the persistence of the hot and humid \nconditions through Monday, as the upper-level ridge remains in \nplace across the West Central Texas. Overnight lows Sunday night\ninto Monday morning will be in the mid to upper 70s, with some \nareas nearing 80 in the western and central portions of the Big \nCountry. High temperatures on Monday will range in the mid 90s to \naround 105, with heat index values in the triple digits across \nmuch of the area. A Heat Advisory will likely be required on \nMonday for portion of the Big Country, Concho Valley, and \nHeartland with heat index values in the 105 to 108 degree range. \nHowever, there is some potential for portions of the western Big \nCountry and western Concho Valley reaching up to 110 degrees, \nwhich may require a Extreme Heat Warning. We will continue to \nmonitor the situation for heat hazards on Monday. \n\nUpper level ridge builds across the Southwest US for mid week,\ncreating a little more northwest flow aloft. A shortwave\ndisturbance tracking through the area from the north Monday  \nafternoon/evening into Tuesday could provide enough support to \ninitiate convection over portions of the Big Country. Best\nprecipitation chances for the Big Country will be Monday night \ninto early Tuesday morning. With a similar setup for Tuesday and\nWednesday, there are low to medium chances for showers and\nisolated thunderstorms again Tuesday and Wednesday afternoon and\nevening Temperatures remain hot through the rest of the week, \nbut models do suggest a little drying as wet soils dry out. This \nmay be enough to drop heat index values back down to roughly match\nactual temperatures and below heat advisory criteria.",
